    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "All **261 tests pass** \u2014 `BUILD SUCCESS`, including the new `AcceptanceTest.coreNumericLevel` (asserting `membershipLevel == 3`) and the 28 `OwnerRestControllerV1Tests`. (The `ERROR`/stack-trace lines in the log are a pre-existing test that deliberately exercises an error path; all tests report 0 failures/errors.)\n\n## Summary\n\nI replaced the string membership tier with a numeric `membershipLevel` (1\u20133), touching four places:\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 Replaced the `membershipTier` string enum (`BRONZE`/`SILVER`/`GOLD`) property with a `membershipLevel` `integer` (int32, min 1, max 3). This regenerates `OwnerDto` with `membershipLevel`/`getMembershipLevel()` and drops `membershipTier`/`MembershipTierEnum`.\n\n2. **`OwnerMapper.java`** \u2014 Swapped the tier-derivation expression for the level formula: `min(3, 1 + (email present ? 1 : 0) + (namesakeCount == 0 ? 1 : 0))`, null-safe on both fields. Computed in the mapper so both `GET` and create responses carry it.\n\n3. **`OwnerRestControllerV1.java`**:\n   - Removed the `applyGoldTier(...)` helper and its `HOUSEHOLD_GOLD_THRESHOLD` constant, plus both call sites (the GOLD promotion operated on the now-removed `membershipTier`). Household-id assignment itself is untouched.\n   - Updated the create **audit line** to record the level: it now maps the DTO first and logs `membershipLevel={}` alongside id/customerCode/registrationDate.\n\n### Notes on interpretation\n- The spec's \"level 4 is reserved for tenure\" is documented in the schema but not implemented \u2014 nothing in the spec assigns level 4 yet, and the cap is 3.\n- The spec says \"anything that recorded the tier (such as the create audit line) must record the level instead.\" The existing audit line didn't actually contain the tier, so I added `membershipLevel` to it to satisfy the requirement that the create audit line record the level. No test asserts the audit format, so this is a faithful-to-spec choice rather than a test-driven one \u2014 worth confirming if you had a specific audit format in mind.",
